@page "/health"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@implements IDisposable
@inject ChargeControlStatusHolder StatusHolder
@inject WebBuildInfo Build
@inject TimeProvider Time
@inject IServiceShutdown Shutdown

@*
    The whole of phase 0's user interface. It exists to prove the two things no unit test can: that a
    component in this assembly is reached by the host's dependency injection, and that it sees the
    same live objects the polling loop and the MQTT worker do.

    Everything on the page comes out of that container -- the build version, the app's configured
    time zone, and the status holder the poll loop writes to. Because the page subscribes to the
    holder's Updated event rather than reading it once, each completed poll pushes a new value down
    the circuit: the timestamp below advances on its own, and a page that sits still is telling you
    the poll loop has stopped while the web host is fine.
*@

<PageTitle>Health — Gleanvolt</PageTitle>

<h1>Health</h1>

<dl class="facts">
    <dt>Build</dt>
    <dd>@Build.Version</dd>

    <dt>Time zone</dt>
    <dd>@Time.LocalTimeZone.Id</dd>

    <dt>Last poll</dt>
    @if (_status is null)
    {
        <dd class="muted">no poll has completed yet</dd>
    }
    else
    {
        <dd>
            @TimeZoneInfo.ConvertTime(_status.Timestamp, Time.LocalTimeZone).ToString("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss")
            <span class="muted">(@Age(_status.Timestamp) ago)</span>
        </dd>
    }

    <dt>Charge mode</dt>
    <dd>@(_status is null ? "—" : _status.Mode.ToString())</dd>
</dl>

@*
    Stopping the service belongs on this page rather than the dashboard: it is not a charging
    decision, it is the process itself, alongside the build and the poll it reports on.

    Two clicks, not one. The stop is graceful and loses nothing -- that is the whole reason it exists
    -- but it is one-way from here: the page it is on goes down with the service, so starting it
    again means an ssh session on the Pi. A confirmation is the cheapest way to make sure the click
    was meant, and the text spells out what "stopped" costs.
*@
<section class="controls">
    <h2>Service</h2>

    <div class="control">
        @if (_stopping)
        {
            <p class="stopping">
                Stopping. The charger is being paused and the open session closed; this page will stop
                responding in a few seconds.
            </p>
            <span class="hint">
                Start it again on the Pi with <code>docker compose start gleanvolt-controller</code> in
                <code>/opt/gleanvolt</code>. Nothing restarts it by itself — that is what makes this a stop
                rather than a restart.
            </span>
        }
        else if (_confirming)
        {
            <label>Stop the service?</label>
            <button type="button" class="danger" @onclick="ConfirmStop">Yes, stop it</button>
            <button type="button" class="secondary" @onclick="() => _confirming = false">Cancel</button>
            <span class="hint">
                Charging stops, this UI and the Home Assistant integration go offline, and the
                controller stays down until it is started again from a shell on the Pi.
            </span>
        }
        else
        {
            <label>Stop the service</label>
            <button type="button" class="danger" @onclick="() => _confirming = true">Stop service</button>
            <span class="hint">
                The safe way to take the controller down: the charger is returned to its pause current,
                the open charging session is closed and written, and the session store is flushed —
                none of which happens if the process is killed instead. It does not come back on its
                own.
            </span>
        }
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    private ChargeControlStatus? _status;
    private bool _confirming;
    private bool _stopping;

    // The request returns immediately -- the host stops on its own thread -- so this render, and the
    // "stopping" message, actually reach the browser before the circuit goes away.
    private void ConfirmStop()
    {
        _confirming = false;
        _stopping = true;
        Shutdown.RequestStop("Web UI");
    }

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Seeded before subscribing, so a browser that arrives between two polls still sees the last
        // one rather than an empty page until the next.
        _status = StatusHolder.Current;
        StatusHolder.Updated += OnStatusUpdated;
    }

    // Raised on the polling loop's thread. InvokeAsync hops onto the renderer's synchronisation
    // context, which is what makes touching component state here safe.
    private void OnStatusUpdated(ChargeControlStatus status)
    {
        _status = status;
        InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }

    // The holder is a singleton and outlives every circuit, so an unsubscribed component would be
    // kept alive by its own event handler for as long as the process runs.
    public void Dispose() => StatusHolder.Updated -= OnStatusUpdated;

    // Clamped at zero: a clock stepped backwards by NTP must not read as a poll from the future.
    private string Age(DateTimeOffset timestamp)
    {
        var age = Time.GetUtcNow() - timestamp;
        if (age < TimeSpan.Zero)
        {
            age = TimeSpan.Zero;
        }

        return age.TotalSeconds < 90
            ? $"{age.TotalSeconds:F0} s"
            : $"{age.TotalMinutes:F0} min";
    }
}
